<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Colony of Curaçao</h1><p class="st-meta">1634 CE – 1954 CE · capital Willemstad</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Dutch island colony that served as the Dutch West India Company's main Caribbean trading hub and slave depot, and later as capital of all Dutch Antillean territories.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> captured <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/curacao">Curaçao</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1634 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-van-walbeeck">Johannes van Walbeeck</a>, seeking a naval base, salt and a foothold near the Spanish Main. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aruba">Aruba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonaire">Bonaire</a> were taken soon after. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-stuyvesant">Peter Stuyvesant</a>, governor from 1642 before he moved to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-netherland">New Netherland</a>, the island became a company stronghold around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willemstad">Willemstad</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dry soil was poor for plantations, so <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/curacao">Curaçao</a> lived by trade, both legal and smuggled, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>. It was a main transit market for enslaved Africans in the 17th and 18th centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willemstad">Willemstad's</a> merchant community included one of the oldest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sephardic-jews">Sephardic Jewish</a> congregations in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikve-israel-emanuel-synagogue">Mikvé Israel</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papiamento">Papiamentu</a> emerged as the common language. A major slave revolt led by Tula broke out in 1795.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> occupied the island during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, returning it in 1816. From 1845 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willemstad">Willemstad</a> governed all six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> islands as Curaçao and Dependencies. The Shell oil refinery, opened in 1918, transformed the economy. In 1954 the colony became the autonomous <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/netherlands-antilles">Netherlands Antilles</a> within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/colony-of-curacao.jpg" alt="Colony of Curaçao" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Netherlands.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1634 CE – 1954 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Willemstad</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Johannes van Walbeeck, Peter Stuyvesant</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, Dutch Reformed Church and Judaism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dutch and Papiamentu</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Curaçao, Aruba, Sint Maarten, Netherlands</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Curaçao and Dependencies, Dutch Curaçao</dd></dl></aside></div>
